When rebuilding an existing endpoint, I get a few things first:

Note that if the boss VM is not running, start it.

Log into the control node and copy the rfmonitor config
to Mothership boss and put it in ~stoller/powder/systems/XXX

    control> scp /etc/rfmonitor/device_config.json \
	boss.emulab.net:/home/stoller/powder/systems/XXX/rfmon.json

On the boss, grab interfaces info and copy to Mothership boss and put
it in ~stoller/powder/systems/XXX

    boss> echo 'select node_id,iface,role,ip,mac from interfaces;' | \
	mysql -t tbdb > /tmp/iface.txt
    boss> scp /tmp/iface.txt boss.emulab.net:/home/stoller/powder/systems/XXX/

Grab a copy of the DB and copy to Mothership boss and put it in
~stoller/powder/systems/XXX

    boss> mysqldump --skip-opt tbdb | gzip > /tmp/tbdb.sql.gz
    boss> scp /tmp/tbdb.sql.gz boss.emulab.net:/home/stoller/powder/systems/XXX/

If you want to retain the older RF monitor files, capture them now as well:

    boss> cd /usr/testbed/www
    boss> sudo tar czf /tmp/rfmonitor.tar.gz rfmonitor
    boss> scp /tmp/rfmonitor.tar.gz boss.emulab.net:/home/stoller/powder/systems/XXX/

Now use the interfaces info to update the files in ~stoller/powder/systems/XXX
(currently only nodes.txt gets updated for the ed1 node ctrl/mgmt MACs).
If the node.txt is space delimited, needs to be fixed up. Look at one of the
other buses.

Before removing the old disk from the endpoint

    msboss> manage_aggregate EBC chflag admin yes

Before starting the elabinelab:

    msboss> sudo /usr/testbed/sbin/protogeni/cacontrol -R -r boss.<aggregate>.powderwireless.net

Now you can handle the machinations associated with reading the disk (NVMe)
for reload. What I have been doing is saving the old disk (that you just got
info off of) and using one of the old disk from a bus you have previously
upgraded, preferably one that has booted from its new disk and is running
with no problems.

Once you have the new target disk, switch nuc20 back into UEFI BIOS mode
and boot it into the admin MFS. Then I completely erase the disk with:

    /usr/sbin/zapdisk64 -EZ /dev/nvme0n1

The console will show some abort errors, but those don't seem to indicate
any real issue. You can run the zapdisk64 again if you are concerned.

Now you can free nuc20 from whatever experiment it is in, to run through
reloading and get it free once more.

Now update the ns file for experiment elab-febuild (or elab-mebuild2).
You need to change:

	CONFIG_NICKNAME (.defs files POWDER_NICKNAME value)
	TARGETSYS_DOMAIN
	TARGETSYS_NETWORK
	TARGETSYS_ROUTER
	TARGETSYS_BOSSIP
	TARGETSYS_HOMEBASE (where does this come from?)
	SSLCERT_ORGNAME
	XEN_STARTUPSCRIPT (rc.powder argument first arg should be router IP)


------

Fire up the elabinelab.

[
HACK HACK! Until the Xen 4.16 image is updated, you will have to manually
intervene as the FBSD135-64-STD boss VM is booting. During the initial boot,
it will stop at the "mountroot> " prompt because we have the wrong root
device. You have to catch this within a couple of minutes of it happening
or else `stated` will declare failure and the swapin will fail. To catch
this prompt, you have to wait til nuc20 has been reloaded with Xen and
starts to setup the boss VM. I usually do this by logging into nuc20 when
it comes up and monitoring the setup of the VM:

    tail -f /var/emulab/logs/tbvnode-nucvm20-1.log

It will run frisbee twice, once to download the FBSD image, the other to
create a "golden image" from that. After that second frisbee, I start
watching for the VM console to become functional. From boss (not the node!)
do:

    wap console nucvm20-1

and keep repeating every few seconds til it stops complaining about
"Could not find tipserver info for nucvm20-1" and connects. Then you will
see the VM booting. Once it stops at the mountroot prompt you type:

    ufs:/dev/da0p3

and it should continue booting. But the horror show is not over yet.

After the VM comes up you can ssh in from boss as root and start watching
the /etc/fstab file. You will see that the root device is "ada0" when it
should be "da0", but you cannot change it too soon or it will get
overwritten! Wait til:

  # the following extra FS device added by /usr/local/etc/emulab/mkextrafs.pl
  /dev/da2p1 /usr/testbed ufs rw 0 2

gets added and then you can do:

    ed /etc/fstab
    2s/ada/da
    w
    q

Eventually, a third line gets added, but you must do it before that lines
is added.

We now return you to your regularly scheduled setup instructions...
]

Once the VMs are ready, copy the directory mentioned above over to the
inner boss as /usr/testbed/etc/targetsys. Do NOT put this stuff on the
inner ops!

    msboss> sudo scp -rp ~stoller/powder/systems/XXX \
	nucvm20-1:/usr/testbed/etc/targetsys/

Sync the apt package mirror. You need the msboss elabman ssh key in your agent. 

    msboss> ~stoller/testbed/testbed/install/powder-fixed/syncmirror.sh \
	nucvm20-1

Now we convert the VMs for boot in the target environment:

First ssh (as root) into inner ops:

    ops> cd /usr/testbed/obj/testbed/install
    ops> perl emulab-install -b -i ops/targetsys ops
    ops> perl emulab-install -b -i ops/powder-endpoint ops

Now ssh (as root) into the inner boss VM from outer boss:

    boss> cd /usr/testbed/obj/testbed/install
    boss> perl emulab-install -b -i boss/targetsys boss
    boss> cd /users/elabman/emulab-devel/obj/install
    boss> perl emulab-install -b -i boss/powder-endpoint boss

Add these to both inner boss/ops /etc/mail/aliases and run newaliases.
(NOTE: On boss, you will be replacing existing alises.)
----

# Send everything to the Mothership.
testbed-ops:            powder-aggregate-ops@emulab.net
testbed-logs:           powder-aggregate-logs@emulab.net
testbed-www:            powder-aggregate-www@emulab.net
testbed-approval:       powder-aggregate-approval@emulab.net
testbed-audit:          powder-aggregate-audit@emulab.net
testbed-stated:         powder-aggregate-stated@emulab.net
testbed-testsuite:      powder-aggregate-testsuite@emulab.net
testbed-errors:         powder-aggregate-errors@emulab.net
testbed-automail:       powder-aggregate-automail@emulab.net

----
Grab inner boss:/usr/testbed/etc/elabman.pswd and save it to boss:

    msboss> sudo scp -p nucvm20-1:/usr/testbed/etc/elabman.pswd \
	~stoller/powder/systems/xxx/elabman.pswd

Fix for ntpdate:
    boss> echo '155.98.33.74    ntp1.emulab.net ntp1' | \
	sudo bash -c '/bin/cat >> /etc/hosts'

Copy MFS tar ball to boss while the network is good:

    msboss> sudo scp ~mike/FE.tar.gz nucvm20-1:/tftpboot

Now we have to shutdown the VMs. Log out of the boss/ops vms, and log
into the physical host and then:

    vhost-0> sudo /usr/local/etc/emulab/vnodesetup -jh nucvm20-1

Make sure its really gone, then toss the token to David.

While waiting for the token, approve the certificate on the MotherShip.

	msboss> sudo /usr/testbed/sbin/protogeni/cacontrol -c boss.<aggregate>.powderwireless.net
	msboss> sudo /usr/local/etc/rc.d/2.portalpsd.sh stop
	msboss> sudo /usr/local/etc/rc.d/2.portalpsd.sh start

If a brand new install, add the named entries to bas and ddc named server.
	
	bas> sudo /usr/local/etc/rc.d/named restart
	ns5> sudo /usr/local/etc/rc.d/named restart

Wait wait wait, for the token from David.
After David does his magic and boss boots then:

Log into the new control node and remove the symlink in /etc/xen/auto,
  [ If "control.<aggregate>.powderwireless.net" does not resolve
    on boss when you try to ssh in, login using the IP address
    and startup the boss VM (next step). Then you should be
    able to ssh in using the DNS name. If the control node has
    not been up for a very long time, it times out in the DNS. ]

We wil put ths back once the endpoint is installed and things are working
okay. Saves on filesystem corruptions.

	control> sudo rm /etc/xen/auto/boss.conf

But start the VM if it is not running. When building an ME, it will
not be running:

	control> sudo xl create /etc/xen/boss.conf

Then login to boss.

Add the special interface that allows monitoring to work:

    	# ME
	boss> wap addinterface -t P2PLTE -e expt ed1 rf0
	OR
	# FE
	boss> wap addinterface -t P2PLTE -e expt nuc2 rf0

And some other tidbits that should be automated:

	boss> wap update_sitevars 
	boss> wap addservers
        boss> sudo getcacerts -f
	boss> sudo /bin/rm -rf `cat /users/elabman/emulab-devel/emulab-devel/install/powder-fixed/rmfiles.txt`
	boss> sudo mv /etc/emulab /etc/emulab.old
	boss> sudo mv /usr/local/etc/emulab /usr/local/etc/emulab.old
	boss> sudo testbed-control boot

Unpack the MFSs:

	boss> cd /tftpboot
	boss> sudo tar xzf FE.tar.gz
	boss> cd NEW
	boss> sudo sh install.sh

Apply the boss/ops SSL patch. There are now two versions of this patch, one for
an older -p2 patch level of 13.5 and one for the newer -p14 version. You can
find out which version is running with

        boss> freebsd-version

It should show either 13.5-RELEASE-p2 or 13.5-RELEASE-p14. Use "p2" or "p14" in
the scp below.

        boss> scp boss.emulab.net:~mike/ssl-fix-<VERS>.tar.gz /tmp/ssl-fix.tar.gz
	boss> tar -C /tmp -xzf /tmp/ssl-fix.tar.gz
	boss> cd /tmp/ssl-fix; sudo sh fixssl.sh
	boss> sudo rm -rf /tmp/ssl-fix*

Re-install syncthing.
	Follow the instructions in powder/syncthing/README in the source tree.
	
Copy the defs file back to the MotherShip.

	boss> cd ~elabman/emulab-devel
	boss> cp defs-targetsys emulab-devel/powder-defs/XXX    (bus-5175)
	boss> scp defs-targetsys boss.emulab.net:/home/stoller/testbed-noelvin/testbed/powder-defs/XXX
	boss> sudo rm defs-targetsys
	boss> sudo -u elabman ln -s emulab-devel/powder-defs/XXX defs-targetsys

Be sure to commit the new defs file.

Do the letsencrypt dance:

	boss> cd ~elabman/emulab-devel/obj/install
	boss> sudo perl emulab-install -b -i letsencrypt boss

Shutdown the boss VM and wait until it is installed.

