After installation in the endpoint:

Copy over Jie's monitor:

	msboss> cd ~stoller/testbed-noelvin
	msboss> sudo rsync -av -C rfmonitor-jie control.<aggregate>.powderwireless.net:/users/stoller

Install Jie's monitor:

	control> cd ~stoller/rfmonitor-jie
	control> sudo systemctl stop rfmonitor
	control> sudo python3 setup.py install
	control> (cd /usr/bin; sudo rm -f rfmonitor; sudo ln -s /usr/local/bin/rfmonitor .)

Setup the rfmonitor config file. Either copy it from the old disk backup
(~stoller/powder/systems/XXX/rfmon.json), or create a new one by creating
/etc/rfmonitor/device_config.json:

  Bus:
	{
	    "devices" : {
	      "ed1:rf0"  : {
	        "name" : "ed1:rf0",
	        "channels" : {"0" : "TX/RX", "1" : "TX/RX"},
	        "direction" : {"incident" : "0", "transmit" : "1"}
	      }
	    }
	}

   Endpoint:
	{
	    "devices" : {
	      "nuc2:rf0"  : {
	        "name" : "nuc2:rf0",
	        "channels" : {"0" : "TX/RX", "1" : "TX/RX"},
	        "direction" : {"incident" : "0", "transmit" : "1"}
	      }
	    }
	}

    About direction: Flip a coin. Heads, set incident to 0 and transmit to 1.
    Tails, set incident to 1 and transmit to 0.

Run the noise calibration. I have no idea what this does:

	control> cd ~stoller/rfmonitor-jie
	control> (cd scripts; sudo ./run_noisefloor_calibration.sh)

Start the monitor. Hopefully you see it in ps and it is not in a death loop.

	control> sudo systemctl start rfmonitor
	control> sudo journalctl -f -u rfmonitor

Start up the boss VM:

	control> sudo xl create /etc/xen/boss.conf

Make sure the monitor reports are coming in every couple of minutes:

	boss> tail -f /usr/testbed/log/rfmonitor_daemon.log

Enable the deadman radio watcher:

       boss> wap setsitevar powder/deadman_enable 1

Make sure the arduino is doing the right thing:

	boss> tail -f /usr/testbed/log/endpoint-monitor.log

	[ If it says it cannot connect, make sure that 'powduino'
	  is in the tiplines table in the DB and capture is running. ]

    On an FE:
	Oct 28 13:42:33 : keepalive: 1761680553
	Oct 28 13:42:33 : New tempF: 66.650
	Oct 28 13:42:33 : temp:66.5564
	Oct 28 13:42:47 : keepalive: 1761680567
	Oct 28 13:42:47 : New tempF: 66.650
	Oct 28 13:42:47 : temp:66.5834

    On an ME:
	Oct 28 13:45:11 : New tempF: 89.492
	Oct 28 13:45:11 : temp:89.4164, onbat:0, voltage:12.084, current:7.380, v':12.409
	Oct 28 13:45:25 : keepalive: 1761680725
	Oct 28 13:45:25 : New tempF: 89.492
	Oct 28 13:45:25 : temp:89.429, onbat:0, voltage:12.182, current:5.327, v':12.416

Free the nodes from hwdown:

	# FE
	boss> wap nfree emulab-ops hwdown nuc1 nuc2

        # ME
	boss> wap nfree emulab-ops hwdown ed1

Now for the fun part! Start this experiment in the emulab-ops project:
Be sure to select correct radio or FE or ME. Delete the spectrum range!

	https://www.powderwireless.net/show-profile.php?profile=6328aa9e-7c99-11e9-8a5c-e4434b2381fc

On boss start this up:

	tail -f /usr/testbed/log/rfmonitor_daemon.log

And log into the experiment node and run:

	node> /usr/libexec/uhd/examples/tx_waveforms --ant TX/RX --channel 0 --rate 1e6 --freq 3550e6 --gain 50

    Let it run while watching the output from rfmonitor_daemon.log. It might
    take til the second report to see anything. Ideally you want to see this:

    	Above floor:-110.0 ed1:rf0: 3550,-104.455875279867,3556.0
	No interface limit info for ed1
	New previous data:
	$VAR1 = {
		  'ed1:rf0:3550' => {
                              'node_id' => 'ed1',
                              'frequency' => '3550.0',
                              'instance' => undef,
                              'incident' => undef,
                              'power' => '-104.45587527986746',
                              'center' => '3556.0',
                              'violation' => 0,
                              'iface' => 'rf0',
                              'repeatcount' => 0,
                              'abovefloor' => '5.54412472013254',
                              'timestamp' => 1761689300
                            }
		};

    If after another iteration or two there is nothing or if you see stuff on
    totally other frequenceies, you can stop. You probably had a bad coin
    flip up above. Reverse incident and transmit and rerun the noise
    calculation, restart the rfmonitor (all this on the control node. Then
    rerun the transmit test. Hopefully you see something then.

Restore symlink in /etc/xen/auto

	control> cd /etc/xen/auto
	control> sudo ln -s ../boss.conf .

Let the bus roam:

	msboss> manage_aggregate Bus6365 chflag admin no

QED


Checking a mobile endpoint.

1. If you cannot resolve {control,boss}.<aggregate>.powderwireless.net
   then it probably means the boss has been down for awhile and the DNS
   cache has timed out. Find the IP address of the control node in
   the source powder-defs/<aggregate> file as CONTROL_ROUTER_IP.
   If that address does not ping, something is wrong with the node.
   If you can ssh into the control node that way, fire up the boss VM:

      sudo xl create /etc/xen/boss.conf

   and follow along:

      sudo console-nossl -l boss

   to make sure it comes up. If it is coming up for the first time in
   a long time, you will want to check the filesystems. So as it is booting,
   wait for it to start loading the kernel text+data and type space to
   interrupt it. When it drops to the boot prompt do:

      boot -s

   This will bring it to single user mode where you can:

      fsck -yf
      reboot
      
   and skip to step 3.
   
2. If the boss node was already up, and you suspect filesystem problems,
   shut the boss down to single user:

     sudo shutdown now

   umount all the filesystems, check/fix the local filesystems, and reboot:

     zfs unmount -a
     accton
     umount -a
     mount -o ro -u /
     fsck -yf
     reboot

3. Once boss comes back, login and see how far the update made it.
   It is safe to assume that we got thru fixing /etc/hosts for "ntp1"
   in the steps above.

   Start by seeing if the MFSes got updated. Check for /tftpboot/FE.tar.gz.
   if that does not exist, you will have to copy it over and installed it
   as described above ("Unpack the MFSs"). If it does exist and the
   /tftpboot/NEW directory is there, the tarball has likely been unpacked
   and installed, skip to the next step. Otherwise, follow the instructions
   above.

4. It is most likely the case that the above has been done. The next couple
   of items happened late in the game after we had already reinstalled most
   of the endpoints. So it is more likely you will need to do these.

   Make sure the SSL install has been fixed. Do:

      ls -lat /usr/lib/ | head

   If the "engines" subdirectory along with the "libcrypto" libraries are
   on top, this patch has been applied. Otherwise follow the instructions
   above ("Apply the boss/ops SSL patch").

5. Make sure syncthing has been reinstalled and activated. This is what
   is used to transfer the RF monitor logs back to boss.
   If /usr/testbed/etc/syncthing exists, then all is well and you are
   done. Other follow the instructions in powder/syncthing/README.
   You do not need to do the initial steps that apply to the mothership.
   Start with step 6 to create a tarball of the needed config, and then
   all the following steps under "To add collection of RF monitoring data".

6. I do the "letsencrypt dance" as well, since it is idempotent and does
   not hurt.

7. Follow the steps to (re)install and test the RF monitor



Problems I have encountered:

0. Reboot errors to ignore on the console.

   Not all errors at reboot time indicate a problem (at least not a problem
   we care about). If you see:

     bad exports list line '/ops/...': symbolic link in export path or statfs failed

   these are due to the funky way we manage ZFS in the ops jail. Nothing to
   see here. When apache starts you will see:

      [core:warn] [pid 2447] (2)No such file or directory: AH00075: Failed to enable the 'httpready' Accept Filter

   for both "httpready" and "dataready". Those are modules we don't bother
   loading in the OS. Just move along.

1. Repeated reboots

   Often this is signaled by a steady stream of emails from stated saying
   it is starting up, with no intervening messages about shutting down.
   This most likely indicates a corrupt filesystem. To fix:

     ssh control.<aggregate>.powderwireless.net
     sudo console-nossl -l boss

   If it is unresponsive at the console (no login prompt, no echo), then
   you can get out of console and:

     sudo xl shutdown boss

   Then get back into the console and wait for it to shutdown to single user.

   If boss is responsive (i.e., login prompt), then you need to reboot it.
   ssh in from another window and shut the boss down to single user:

     ssh boss.<aggregate>.powderwireless.net
     sudo shutdown now

   Umount all the filesystems, check/fix the local filesystems, and reboot:

     zfs unmount -a
     accton
     umount -a
     mount -o ro -u /
     fsck -yf
     reboot

2. "Could not generate CRL" and "Failed: postcr"

   Probably complains about inability to parse the lastupdate field.
   Usually due to a current or past corruption in the FS that leads to an
   empty crl.pem file.

   Make sure you have the console just in case it is a current corruption
   and crashes while you are fixing this.
     
     cd /usr/testbed/ssl
     sudo rm crl.pem
     sudo /usr/testbed/sbin/protogeni/gencrl
     sudo /usr/testbed/sbin/protogeni/postcrl

3. Control node is up, but not the boss VM

   First, login to the control node and make sure that
   /etc/xen/auto/boss.conf exists (symlink to "../boss.conf". If not,
   we probably didn't finish the in-the-field testing. Most likely the
   test to see if the RF monitor is working. Follow the "Checking"
   process above.

   If that file exists but the boss VM is not running, then it probably
   did not pass the conditions required by the "start gate"
   /usr/local/bin/xendomains-start-gate.sh. Run:

      /usr/local/bin/get_arduino_data.py -v

   and it should report three numbers:

     onbattery,voltage,onacc

   which must meet the conditions in /etc/default/xendomains-custom.
   Typically this means: not on battery, not on ACC, and voltage > 11.
   If it is on battery, then the bus is not powered on at all.
   If it is on ACC, then the bus is running off its battery (accessory).
   If both are off and the voltage is low, there might be a problem with
   the power board or our battery. See this thread:

   https://fluxgroup.slack.com/archives/GQ6KJ3K98/p1761684034448029?thread_ts=1761680844.852999&cid=GQ6KJ3K98

   Or search for DATA_OPTIONS in the Slack search.

   If you get errors from the get_arduino_data.py script, it could be that
   the arduino is bad (though I have not seen this myself).

   If you get: "/dev/ttyACM0 does not exist" (this I have seen), then see if
   the Arduino is seen at all. Do:

     lsusb | grep -i arduino

   and see if anything shows up. If it does then it is likely that the boss
   VM is running (the VM takes control of the Arduino, hence the error).
   First do "xl list" to see if the boss VM exists. Then see if it is all the
   way by trying to login. If not, grab the boss console (as described above)
   and see what it shows. Most likely there was an fsck error that caused it
   to stop in single user mode.

4. Database won't start on boss.

   Most likely you will find out about this via an email saying something
   about a service not starting/running, e.g., "wbstoremonitor died".

   If you login to the boss VM and cannot connect to the DB with "mysql tbdb"
   then do a ps and see if there are any mysql (mariadb) processes running.
   If not, check in /usr/testbed/log/mysqld-error.log and see what happened.
   You will most likely see something like:

     File '/usr/testbed/log/mysql/update.000106' not found

   which indicates that the node died at an inopportune time when it had
   added that file to the update.index but not written the file. Edit
   /usr/testbed/log/mysql/update.index and remove that update file from
   the end. Then:

     /usr/local/etc/rc.d/2.mysql-server.sh
     mysqlcheck --repair --all-databases

   To be extra safe, reboot and watch the console for other errors because
   this most like happened due to filesystem errors.
